---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import GuideLayout from "../../components/GuideLayout.astro";
import GuideRaceDemo from "../../components/GuideRaceDemo.astro";
import { watershedCodeTheme } from "../../styles/code-theme";
import { excerpt } from "../../lib/excerpt";
import convergenceSource from "../../../../examples/retro_tutorial_lustre/test/convergence_test.gleam?raw";

const addRace = excerpt(
  convergenceSource,
  "pub fn concurrent_adds_keep_both_notes_test(",
);

const voteRace = excerpt(
  convergenceSource,
  "pub fn concurrent_plus_plus_minus_votes_settle_at_plus_one_test(",
);
---

<GuideLayout
  slug="race"
  description="Step one of the watershed build guide: watch what happens when two people add a note at the same instant, and when three votes land on one note at once — then read the tests that prove both come out right."
>
  <GuideRaceDemo slot="wide" />

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Start from the thing that breaks</h2>
    <p>
      Most build guides start with a connection string. This one starts with the
      two moments where a shared retro board stops being a plain list — because
      now two people can touch it at the same time, over the network, and
      nobody is standing there to say who went first. Every sheet after this
      one exists to survive one of those two moments.
    </p>
    <p>
      The rig above runs two real <code>watershed</code> documents against the
      <strong>sluice</strong>, a stand-in for the network that lets you hold
      messages back and release them by hand instead of at network speed.
      Nothing here is faked: it’s the same map writes you’ll write in step 03
      and step 04, running through the same client code, just slowed down
      enough to watch.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Race one: the tie nobody wins</h2>
    <p>
      Two people type into <em>Went well</em> in the same second and hit Add.
      Both notes are new, neither one knows the other exists, and there’s no
      good answer to “which one did they mean?” — because they meant both.
    </p>
    <p>
      Store the board as a plain map keyed by column, and you get one note
      back, not two. The map isn’t broken: a map with one key per column only
      has one slot per column, so the second note simply overwrites the first
      — that’s what “last write wins” means. The fix isn’t a smarter merge,
      it’s a better key. Give every note its own id in an <strong>OR-map</strong>{" "}
      — a map built so that two different keys can never collide — and the two
      adds aren’t even in conflict with each other.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Race two: the vote you can’t find afterwards</h2>
    <p>
      Now one standing note, three votes in flight: Client A upvotes, Client B
      upvotes, Client B changes their mind and downvotes. Three separate clicks,
      and only one honest total should come out the other end.
    </p>
    <p>
      Read the count, add one, write the count back — and you’ve just written a
      bug that trips up almost every shared counter: both clients read
      <code>0</code>, both write <code>1</code>, and one vote is gone with
      nothing to show for it — no error, no warning, just a wrong number.
      A <strong>tally</strong> avoids this by sending the change instead of the
      new total — it ships <code>+1</code>, not <code>1</code>. Adding numbers
      doesn’t care what order they show up in, so it stops mattering who the
      server heard from first — the arithmetic lands the same either way. The
      demo settles at <code>+1</code> however you run it.
    </p>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>Want it staged even more sharply?</strong> The{" "}
        <a href="/counter-bug">counter bug</a> page runs the losing version
        first, on purpose, then fixes it. It’s the same argument with the
        failure left in.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Both races are already checked in</h2>
    <p>
      A demo you can’t re-run is a claim, not evidence. Both races above are
      tests in the tutorial project, and they run under <code>gleam test</code>
      in about a second with no server anywhere. Step 06 is where you write
      them; here they’re just proof that the rest of the procedure has to hold
      up.
    </p>
    <span class="g-file">test/convergence_test.gleam</span>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={addRace} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
      <figcaption class="annot">retro_tutorial_lustre · add race</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={voteRace} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
      <figcaption class="annot">retro_tutorial_lustre · vote race</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Both start with the same <code>room</code> helper: one sluice, two
      connected documents, a title, a register OR-map, a tally OR-map. Step 06
      unpacks it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>What you’re about to build</h2>
    <p>
      You build one small application, <strong>retro_tutorial_lustre</strong>,
      across all six sheets. Three fixed columns, sticky notes with a vote count,
      and a roster showing who is reading which note. Every step adds one
      capability to the same source tree, and every step ends somewhere you can run.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code
        code={`document · retro-tutorial
├── title           String field           the board name, "Sprint retro"
├── notes           OR-map · RegisterMode  note id → the whole note as JSON
├── votes           OR-map · TallyMode     note id → a signed total
└── ↯ focused_note  presence               who is reading which note`}
        lang="text"
        theme={watershedCodeTheme}
      />
    </figure>
    <p>
      It stays deliberately small. There are no sequences, no drag and drop, no
      cross-column moves, no edit or delete, and no vote budgets. When you want
      those, the <a href="/examples#retro_board_lustre">advanced retro board</a>
      {" "}is the same idea with five channels and the harder interactions.
    </p>
    <p>
      You’ll need the <a href="https://gleam.run/getting-started/">Gleam
      toolchain</a> and, from step 02 on, a{" "}
      <a href="https://floodgate.tylerbutler.com">floodgate</a> server. The last
      sheet needs neither a server nor a browser.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-out">
    <span class="annot">You can now</span>
    <p>
      Run <code>cd examples/retro_tutorial_lustre && gleam test</code> and watch
      <code>8 passed, no failures</code> pin both claims before you build the
      rest of the board.
    </p>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
